        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="identify" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Which Small Black Flying Bug Is It? Start With the ID Table
          </h2>

          <p>
            Catch one first. A strip of clear packing tape pressed over a resting fly, or a yellow sticky card left near where they gather overnight, gives you a specimen you can look at under a magnifying glass or a zoomed phone camera. Most of these insects look like generic black specks in the air, and they only become distinguishable up close: fuzzy wings or clear ones, long dangling legs or short ones, a pinched waist or a thick one, straight antennae or bent.
          </p>

          <p>
            Behavior narrows it almost as fast as appearance. Where do they rest? Hovering over houseplants and walking on the soil points to fungus gnats. Sitting motionless on bathroom walls and the side of the tub points to drain flies. Running in quick, jerky bursts across counters and floors, often in large numbers, points to phorid flies. Gathering suddenly at windows and lights, especially in spring, points to flying ants or termites.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
